# Cursor Rules (legacy file)
#
# This file exists so Cursor's Source Control "Generate Commit Message" (✨)
# button picks up our commit conventions. The modern `.cursor/rules/*.mdc`
# and `AGENTS.md` files are NOT read by that button as of 2026-05.
# Keep this file in sync with `.cursor/rules/commit-messages.mdc`.

## Commit Message Rules

All commits MUST follow Conventional Commits in English.

### Format

```
<type>(<scope>): <subject>

[optional body]

[optional footer]
```

- Subject: imperative mood, lowercase start, no trailing period, ≤ 72 chars.
- Body: include ONLY when the change is non-trivial — explain the WHY, not the what. Wrap at 72 chars. Blank line between subject and body.
- Footer: `BREAKING CHANGE: <desc>` or `Refs: #123` when applicable.

### Allowed Types

`feat`, `fix`, `refactor`, `perf`, `docs`, `style`, `test`, `build`, `ci`, `chore`, `revert`.

### Suggested Scopes

`accounts`, `auth`, `oauth`, `snapshot`, `menubar`, `ui`, `launch`, `backup`, `build`, `script`, `docs`.

Omit the scope when redundant or when the change spans the whole app.

### Examples

```
feat(menubar): add quick switch shortcut for saved accounts
fix(oauth): handle expired refresh token before switching
refactor(accounts): extract snapshot rotation into AccountStore
docs: update ROADMAP with quota query milestone
chore(build): bump SwiftPM toolchain pin
```

With body (complex change):

```
fix(snapshot): keep saved snapshot in sync with rotated auth.json

Codex App rotates the refresh token in-place. Re-capture the live bytes
back into the matching saved snapshot, and refuse to switch away from an
account whose token has rotated without a re-snapshot.
```

### Hard Rules When Generating a Commit Message

- ALWAYS output ONLY the commit message — no preamble, no explanation, no code fences.
- ALWAYS pick exactly ONE type prefix from the allowed list above.
- NEVER use vague subjects like "update code", "fix bug", "enhance app", "misc changes".
- NEVER capitalize the subject's first word (after the type prefix).
- NEVER end the subject with a period.
- NEVER exceed 72 characters in the subject line.
- NEVER include a body unless the diff actually warrants a "why" explanation.
- NEVER add tool signatures such as "Generated with ..." or "Co-Authored-By: <bot>".
- NEVER mix unrelated changes — if the diff covers multiple concerns, describe the dominant one and note the rest only if essential.
